{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e053c8a521bd310","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Component: model-registry-operator\n2\t\n3\t## Metadata\n4\t\n5\t- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/model-registry-operator.git\n6\t- **Version**: ee9de1751b533457083fdf4d3a4e25c04908bc4d\n7\t- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n8\t- **Languages**: Go\n9\t- **Deployment Type**: Kubernetes Operator / Controller\n10\t- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6 on 2026-08-13\n11\t\n12\t## Purpose\n13\t\n14\t**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of ModelRegistry, Catalog, and AIHub custom resources, provisioning model-registry service instances with kube-rbac-proxy authentication, PostgreSQL storage, and Gateway API or OpenShift Route ingress. [source: cmd/modelregistry.go:169, internal/controller/modelregistry_controller.go:282, internal/controller/modelcatalog_controller.go:183]\n15\t\n16\t**Detailed**: The model-registry-operator is a controller-runtime-based Kubernetes operator that reconciles three CRDs — ModelRegistry (v1alpha1/v1beta1), Catalog (v1alpha1), and AIHub (v1alpha1) — to provision and manage model registry service instances within the RHOAI platform. For each ModelRegistry CR, the operator creates a Deployment running the model-registry proxy backed by either PostgreSQL or MySQL storage, with an optional kube-rbac-proxy sidecar that enforces SubjectAccessReview-based authorization on the HTTPS port. The operator manages the full resource lifecycle including Services, ServiceAccounts, RBAC Roles/Bindings, Secrets, NetworkPolicies, and ingress resources (Gateway API HTTPRoutes or OpenShift Routes depending on cluster capabilities). The ModelCatalog reconciler similarly provisions a centralized catalog service with its own PostgreSQL instance. The operator detects cluster capabilities at startup — including OpenShift APIs, BYOIDC mode (where user.openshift.io is absent), Gateway API availability, and the platform Auth CRD — to conditionally create platform-specific resources. [source: internal/controller/util.go:36-39, internal/controller/config/defaults.go:197-211, internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:220-272]\n17\t\n18\t## Architectural Analysis\n19\t\n20\tThe model-registry-operator follows a multi-controller pattern with two primary reconcilers — `ModelRegistryReconciler` and `ModelCatalogReconciler` — sharing common resource-management utilities for proxy configuration, RBAC, and ingress. Both controllers use Go templates embedded in `internal/controller/config/templates/` to render Kubernetes manifests, enabling a declarative approach to managed workload configuration.\n21\t\n22\t**Authentication architecture.** Each managed model-registry instance optionally deploys a kube-rbac-proxy sidecar container that terminates TLS and enforces Kubernetes SubjectAccessReview authorization. The proxy checks whether the caller has `get` access to the Service resource in the target namespace, effectively delegating access control to Kubernetes RBAC. This pattern allows namespace-scoped access control through standard RoleBindings (e.g., `registry-user-{registry-name}` grants the `{registry-name}-users` group access to endpoints and services). The operator also creates auth-delegator ClusterRoleBindings for each registry instance. [source: internal/controller/config/templates/kube-rbac-proxy/kube-rbac-proxy-config.yaml.tmpl:17-23, internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:220-272]\n23\t\n24\t**Platform-adaptive ingress.** The operator implements a dual ingress strategy: Gateway API HTTPRoutes for clusters with gateway-api support and OpenShift Routes for backward compatibility. HTTPRoutes reference a configurable Gateway (defaulting to `data-science-gateway`) with path-prefix-based routing and URL rewriting, while Routes provide direct TLS-terminated HTTPS access. The backend port selection is conditional — routing through the kube-rbac-proxy port (typically 8443) when configured, otherwise directly to the rest-container port. [source: internal/controller/config/templates/gateway/gateway-httproute.yaml.tmpl:16-38, internal/controller/modelregistry_oauth.go:73-76]\n25\t\n26\t**Cluster capability detection.** At startup, the operator probes the Kubernetes discovery API to detect available platform features (OpenShift routes, user API, config API, platform Auth CRD), stored in a `ClusterCapabilities` struct. This enables graceful degradation across OpenShift, BYOIDC-enabled OpenShift 4.20+, and plain Kubernetes. The operator reads the cluster-level `config.openshift.io/v1/Ingress` resource to determine the default application domain for Route hostnames. [source: internal/controller/util.go:30-39, internal/setup/setup.go:80-91, internal/controller/config/defaults.go:196-211]\n27\t\n28\t**TLS security posture.** The operator reads the OpenShift cluster TLS security profile via `controller-runtime-common` and applies it to webhook and metrics server TLS configuration, ensuring alignment with cluster-wide TLS policy. The Konflux build uses `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` and `CGO_ENABLED=1` for FIPS-compliant dynamic linking against OpenSSL. [source: internal/setup/setup.go:93-129, Dockerfile.konflux:22]\n29\t\n30\t**CRD version migration.** The operator manages CRD version migration from v1alpha1 to v1beta1 for ModelRegistry via a `StorageVersionMigration` strategy, ensuring backward compatibility during API evolution. The conversion webhook at `/convert` handles live request translation between API versions. [source: internal/migration/svm_strategy.go:122, config/crd/patches/webhook_in_modelregistries.yaml:3]\n31\t\n32\t## Provenance\n33\t\n34\t### Repo Lineage\n35\t\n36\t| Role | Repository | Sync Mechanism | Sync Branch | Sync Workflows | Detection Method |\n37\t|----|----------|--------------|-----------|--------------|----------------|\n38\t| Upstream | https://github.com/opendatahub-io/mo