{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-79f90c1a-48f8-43b0-a968-32e0b45f5841","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0fd5277eb8c1ca7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-rs8r4br_/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.md","content":"# Decide the default vector store provisioning approach for Gen AI Studio\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves how the default pgvector-backed PostgreSQL instance\nis provisioned for Gen AI Studio playground RAG in RHOAI 3.5, so that removing\nthe inline vector providers from the OGX distribution does not break playground\nRAG. It produces the go/no-go decision the Staff Engineer Input requires the\ndevelopment team to define as part of sprint planning: operator-based\nself-provisioning (if the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from the dashboard\noperator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164, lands in time for 3.5), BFF-managed\nprovisioning by the gen-ai BFF, or bring-your-own PostgreSQL only. It also\nfixes the scoping model for the default store (per namespace vs per OGX\ninstance).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define explicit go/no-go criteria for operator-based provisioning in 3.5,\n  tied to the delivery status of the dashboard operator modularization\n  (RHAISTRAT-7164, In Progress), and drive the gate to an outcome.\n- Compare the two self-provisioning options, both following the\n  model-registry-operator's auto-provisioned PostgreSQL pattern:\n  - Operator-based: PostgreSQL lifecycle management lives in the RHOAI-controlled\n    operator layer (the ogx-k8s-operator is upstream and cannot carry\n    RHOAI-specific provisioning logic).\n  - BFF-managed: the gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service,\n    PVC, and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable\n    instance exists in the namespace, similar to how the BFF already provisions\n    guardrails infrastructure.\n- If BFF-managed provisioning is a candidate, produce the explicit lifecycle\n  design the Staff Engineer Input calls out: cleanup on playground deletion,\n  handling of pod restarts (the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation\n  loop), and credential rotation.\n- Evaluate portability requirements: whichever approach is chosen must be\n  designed to consume a platform-provided shared PostgreSQL connection string\n  when it becomes available in RHOAI 3.6, rather than self-provisioning\n  forever.\n- Decide the scoping model — per namespace (preferred for isolation) vs per\n  OGX instance — noting that Auto Rag is out of scope for this strategy.\n- Deliverable: a decision document recording the go/no-go outcome, the selected\n  provisioning approach with rationale, the scoping model, and the enumerated\n  downstream implementation scope for the selected approach.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The decision document records a go/no-go outcome for operator-based\n  provisioning in 3.5, with explicit criteria tied to the dashboard operator\n  modularization delivery status (not an open-ended \"wait and see\").\n- The selected approach is exactly one of: operator-based self-provisioning,\n  BFF-managed provisioning, or bring-your-own PostgreSQL only, with a rationale\n  that compares lifecycle management, portability to the 3.6 platform-shared\n  database, and release risk.\n- If BFF-managed provisioning is selected or kept as the leading fallback, the\n  decision document contains a lifecycle design covering cleanup on playground\n  deletion, pod-restart handling, and credential rotation.\n- The scoping model (per namespace or per instance) is decided with rationale.\n- The downstream implementation scope for the selected approach is enumerated:\n  which resources are created, which configuration surfaces change, and what\n  the acceptance path is for the out-of-box default.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping, e.g. per-namespace isolation): the scoping model\n  is decided here and implemented downstream.\n- SE-2 (portability to the 3.6 platform-shared PostgreSQL): the portability\n  constraint is evaluated and carried into the selected approach's design.\n- Staff directive: define the go/no-go gate for the RHAISTRAT-7164 dependency\n  as part of sprint planning.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Hard external dependency: RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization)\n  is In Progress with timing not controlled by this team; per the Staff\n  Engineer Input, the strategy cannot be committed to a specific release until\n  that dependency is delivered or the fallback is chosen. This epic is the\n  gate that resolves that uncertainty.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":91108,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-91a9c2264e52e415","agent_id":"a119942f9f83e0558","session_id":"a1b0ecca-9f9b-42d4-80d1-54b9096b8411","uuid":"187569f2-f805-45ba-98b1-ed83f36ae780","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:23:51.935Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
