{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-effc11c1-ab27-4fdd-8bf1-474033c4ada7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-abe1855dba9ff0da","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-rs8r4br_/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E003.md","content":"# Validate and benchmark pgvector as the default vector store for playground RAG\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic validates that the remote pgvector provider preserves the current\nGen AI Studio playground RAG capability, and produces the dev-experimentation\nbenchmark and resource sizing that the Staff Engineer Input defines: capture\nthe inline Milvus baseline, verify pgvector is in the same order of magnitude\n(not a specific latency threshold), and derive PostgreSQL resource sizing from\nthe model-registry-operator's reference values. It builds on the pgvector\nprovider configuration capability for the playground.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Capture the inline Milvus provider baseline for representative playground RAG\n  workflows (document upload, embedding, retrieval, generation) before inline\n  providers are removed from the OGX distribution.\n- Benchmark the remote pgvector provider on the same workflows using a flat\n  scan (no index), which is sufficient for typical playground document sets;\n  verify the result is the same order of magnitude as the inline baseline. No\n  latency SLAs (p50/p95/p99) and no corpus-size or index-type selection — the\n  default store is for development experimentation, not production usage.\n- Functionally validate playground RAG workflows end to end against pgvector\n  with no loss of current capability, including the bring-your-own PostgreSQL\n  path.\n- Derive recommended PostgreSQL resource requests/limits for the default\n  instance, starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource\n  values as the reference point and adjusting based on the benchmark data;\n  confirm the default instance is lightweight enough for resource-constrained\n  environments.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The inline Milvus baseline for the representative workflows is captured and\n  recorded (methodology, workloads, results).\n- The benchmark report shows pgvector (flat scan) within the same order of\n  magnitude as the inline baseline for the representative workflows.\n- Each representative playground RAG workflow passes functionally against the\n  remote pgvector provider with no loss of capability compared to the current\n  inline-provider experience.\n- Recommended PostgreSQL resource requests/limits are documented, derived from\n  the model-registry-operator reference and the benchmark data, and are small\n  enough for resource-constrained environments.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- AC-2 (playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no\n  loss of current capability): this epic is the measured verification.\n- AC-6 (lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments without\n  significant overhead): bundled here — it is measured by the same benchmark\n  run and sizing analysis that verify AC-2; splitting it would duplicate the\n  experiment.\n- SE-3 (resource sizing referenced from the model-registry-operator, adjusted\n  by benchmarking).\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The benchmark can run against a manually or user-provisioned PostgreSQL\n  instance; it does not depend on the outcome of the provisioning-approach\n  decision.\n- If pgvector is not within the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus\n  baseline for typical playground usage, the default-store choice must be\n  re-evaluated before inline providers are removed.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":93149,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-91a9c2264e52e415","agent_id":"a119942f9f83e0558","session_id":"a1b0ecca-9f9b-42d4-80d1-54b9096b8411","uuid":"