{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-8ad7c44c-1647-478d-b687-f94cc92ea31f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a942a155fdc48252","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-rs8r4br_/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for Gen AI Studio (fallback path)\n\n## Description\n\nThis branch epic applies when the provisioning-approach decision selects the\nfallback in which the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance\ndirectly — used when the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from the dashboard\noperator modularization is not ready in time for RHOAI 3.5. When a playground\nwith RAG is configured and no suitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the\nnamespace, the BFF creates one, similar to how it already provisions guardrails\ninfrastructure. It builds on the pgvector provider configuration capability in\nthe same BFF surface, supplying the provisioned connection details to it.\n\n## Scope\n\n- The gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret —\n  using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image, which already includes\n  pgvector — when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance\n  exists in the namespace.\n- Implement the lifecycle management the Staff Engineer Input flags as needing\n  explicit design attention, since the BFF is not an operator with a\n  reconciliation loop:\n  - cleanup of provisioned resources on playground deletion (when no other\n    playground in the namespace uses them);\n  - idempotent handling of BFF pod restarts so instances are neither orphaned\n    nor duplicated;\n  - credential rotation for the provisioned instance.\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes\n  available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather\n  than self-provisioning.\n- Wire the provisioned instance's connection details into the playground's\n  OGXServer remote pgvector vector_io provider configuration, scoped per\n  namespace for multi-tenant isolation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Configuring a playground with RAG in a fresh namespace results in a working\n  default vector store with no manual provisioning or configuration steps by\n  the user.\n- The provisioned PostgreSQL runs as a separate process from the OGX instance,\n  scoped per namespace, and the playground's vector traffic goes through the\n  remote pgvector provider.\n- Deleting a playground cleans up the PostgreSQL resources provisioned for it\n  when no other playground in the namespace uses them.\n- A restart of the BFF pod neither orphans nor duplicates the provisioned\n  instance (idempotent reconciliation behavior verified by test).\n- The credential rotation approach is implemented and verified.\n- Switching the BFF to consume a platform-provided shared PostgreSQL\n  connection string (3.6) requires configuration only (portability verified).\n- Resource requests/limits for the provisioned instance follow the agreed\n  reference sizing (model-registry-operator values adjusted by the benchmark\n  results).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual\n  provisioning of external infrastructure).\n- AC-2 (playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no\n  loss of capability) — completes the out-of-box path on top of the provider\n  configuration capability.\n- AC-4 (separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance).\n- AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping, per-namespace isolation).\n- SE-2 (portability to the 3.6 platform-shared PostgreSQL via connection\n  string).\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This path exists only if the provisioning-approach decision selects it as\n  the fallback; if the operator layer lands in time, operator-based\n  self-provisioning is preferred, and if BFF-managed provisioning is deemed\n  too risky, the fallback is bring-your-own PostgreSQL only.\n- The BFF is not an operator: lifecycle correctness (cleanup, restarts,\n  credential rotation) is the principal design risk and is covered by explicit\n  acceptance criteria above.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":94987,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-91a9c2264e52e415","agent_id":"a119942f9f83e0558","session_id":"a1b0ecca-9f9b-42d4-80d1-54b9096b8411","uuid":"a9f8fed7-6c0c-4500-9d7c-2356333c9208","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:24:40.607Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
