{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3c27af958af12b5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E003\n3\ttitle: Validate and benchmark pgvector as the default vector store for playground\n4\t  RAG\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n12\tai_signals:\n13\t  change_specificity: 1\n14\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n15\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n16\t  existing_foundation: 1\n17\t  open_questions: 1\n18\t  external_dependency: 1\n19\t  human_process_gates: 1\n20\t  repo_access: 1\n21\t  architecture_claims: 1\n22\timplementation_type: null\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: High\n25\tai_implementability_score: 7\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: null\n29\tgate_failure_impact: null\n30\t---\n31\t# Validate and benchmark pgvector as the default vector store for playground RAG\n32\t\n33\t## Description\n34\t\n35\tThis epic validates that the remote pgvector provider preserves the current\n36\tGen AI Studio playground RAG capability, and produces the dev-experimentation\n37\tbenchmark and resource sizing that the Staff Engineer Input defines: capture\n38\tthe inline Milvus baseline, verify pgvector is in the same order of magnitude\n39\t(not a specific latency threshold), and derive PostgreSQL resource sizing from\n40\tthe model-registry-operator's reference values. It builds on the pgvector\n41\tprovider configuration capability for the playground.\n42\t\n43\t## Scope\n44\t\n45\t- Capture the inline Milvus provider baseline for representative playground RAG\n46\t  workflows (document upload, embedding, retrieval, generation) before inline\n47\t  providers are removed from the OGX distribution.\n48\t- Benchmark the remote pgvector provider on the same workflows using a flat\n49\t  scan (no index), which is sufficient for typical playground document sets;\n50\t  verify the result is the same order of magnitude as the inline baseline. No\n51\t  latency SLAs (p50/p95/p99) and no corpus-size or index-type selection — the\n52\t  default store is for development experimentation, not production usage.\n53\t- Functionally validate playground RAG workflows end to end against pgvector\n54\t  with no loss of current capability, including the bring-your-own PostgreSQL\n55\t  path.\n56\t- Derive recommended PostgreSQL resource requests/limits for the default\n57\t  instance, starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource\n58\t  values as the reference point and adjusting based on the benchmark data;\n59\t  confirm the default instance is lightweight enough for resource-constrained\n60\t  environments.\n61\t\n62\t## Acceptance Criteria\n63\t\n64\t- The inline Milvus baseline for the representative workflows is captured and\n65\t  recorded (methodology, workloads, results).\n66\t- The benchmark report shows pgvector (flat scan) within the same order of\n67\t  magnitude as the inline baseline for the representative workflows.\n68\t- Each representative playground RAG workflow passes functionally against the\n69\t  remote pgvector provider with no loss of capability compared to the current\n70\t  inline-provider experience.\n71\t- Recommended PostgreSQL resource requests/limits are documented, derived from\n72\t  the model-registry-operator reference and the benchmark data, and are small\n73\t  enough for resource-constrained environments.\n74\t\n75\t## HLR Traceability\n76\t\n77\t- AC-2 (playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no\n78\t  loss of current capability): this epic is the measured verification.\n79\t- AC-6 (lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments without\n80\t  significant overhead): bundled here — it is measured by the same benchmark\n81\t  run and sizing analysis that verify AC-2; splitting it would duplicate the\n82\t  experiment.\n83\t- SE-3 (resource sizing referenced from the model-registry-operator, adjusted\n84\t  by benchmarking).\n85\t\n86\t## Risks / Notes\n87\t\n88\t- The benchmark can run against a manually or user-provisioned PostgreSQL\n89\t  instance; it does not depend on the outcome of the provisioning-approach\n90\t  decision.\n91\t- If pgvector is not within the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus\n92\t  baseline for typical playground usage, the default-store choice must be\n93\t  re-evaluated before inline providers are removed.\n94\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-80d531b12983c1db","agent_id":"abbd3441d532da3f5","session_id":"a1b0ecca-9f9b-42d4-80d1-54b9096b8411","uuid":"8f004467-7258-47e0-a8b0-eff0efd5387e","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:27:10.063Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3c27af958af12b5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
